<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tewa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tewa people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tewa are a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> who speak the Tewa language, a member of the Tanoan family. Their communities along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohkay-owingeh">Ohkay Owingeh</a>, Santa Clara, San Ildefonso, Pojoaque, Nambé and Tesuque, and a Tewa community also lives at Hano on the Hopi mesas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>. The Tewa leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope">Popé</a> organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tewa.jpg" alt="Tewa" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward S. Curtis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_S._Curtis_Collection_People_023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1300 potters on the Rio Grande painted with glazes, and those of the Hopi country made yellow polychrome wares such as the Sikyatki style, which the Hopi-<b>Tewa</b> potter Nampeyo (c. 1859–1942) revived from ancient pottery found at Sikyatki and other old villages.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The name Navajo came through the Spanish from the <b>Tewa</b> language, in which it referred to large cultivated fields; Spanish writers used Apachu de Nabajo in the 1620s and Navajo by the 1640s.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1598 Juan de Oñate led settlers, soldiers and Franciscans up the Camino Real from Mexico and claimed the upper Rio Grande, setting up headquarters at the <b>Tewa</b> pueblo of Ohkay Owingeh.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
